WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.700
Anthropics Auto Mode now defaults for most Claude code users and blocks 89% of dangerous actions in tests where humans only caught 13.6%. This is Models &amp; Agents, episode 136. Anthropic published third-party evaluations showing that Claude models in Auto Mode stopped everyone of 720 indirect prompt injection attempts across 72 scenarios. The same system outperformed human reviewers

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:57.640
at rejecting harmful commands mid-session. Builders should watch how other labs respond to these safety claims and whether independent red teams can reproduce the results. Over the past week, coverage has centered on agent safety benchmarks and enterprise scale rollouts, with teams testing new guardrails against prompt injection and data ex-filtration. Those threads continue today as more labs publish their own evaluation results.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:22.980
Anthropic is making Auto Mode the default for new Claude code sessions on pro, max, and team plans starting August 14. The change follows internal testing and a third-party evaluation by trajectory labs that found zero successful indirect prompt injection attacks against Claude Fable 5, Opus 5, or Sonnet 5 in Auto Mode across 720 attempts.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:45.500
In a separate test with 1053 paid users, Auto Mode blocked 89% of swapped dangerous commands while only 13.6% of humans refused them. Simon Willison notes the remaining 11% failure rate and highlights risks from malicious third-party packages that could still ex-filtrate data even under Auto Mode.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:02:05.360
The evaluation's focus on prompt injection and data ex-filtration, but do not yet cover every possible supply chain attack. Internally, at Anthropic, almost every person already uses Auto Mode and Kat Woo stated that for the main risk categories, the system performs better than average human reviewers.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:20.320
Threek Sheepard described the work as addressing the lethal trifecta of risks. Developers using coding agents should test Auto Mode on non-critical repositories this week and compare it against their current approval workflows.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:37.220
Willison remains concerned that confirmation fatigue in human approval flows is real, and that Auto Mode still leaves room for attacks involving malicious packages such as one that instructs agents to run UVX, fetch-model-files before tests.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:49.460
He has previously predicted a Challenger Disconzaster scale incident for coding agents in 2026 and wants independent confirmation before fully trusting the new safeguards.

00:02:50.380 --> 00:03:02.220
Researchers used Frontier Genome Language Models, EVO-1 and EVO-2 to generate complete viable bacterial phage genomes based on the Phi-X-174 template.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:17.480
The models produce sequences with realistic genetic architecture and targeted host tropism. Experimental validation yielded 16 functional phages that showed substantial evolutionary novelty and had never existed in nature.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:24.160
This marks the first reported case of whole genome generative design that resulted in viable organisms.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:33.060
The work demonstrates that current genome scale language models can now output full-length functional sequences rather than short gene fragments.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:41.240
Teams working on synthetic biology or genome scale modeling should examine the release sequences and the training approach used.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:49.160
Recent evaluations found that current large language models continue to embed racial and gender biases when answering medical questions.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:04:03.980
The study highlights persistent stereotype reinforcement in clinical reasoning tasks, despite safety fine tuning. Researchers at Flinders University documented specific failure patterns across multiple model families.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:10.080
The findings show that bias persists even in models trained with recent alignment techniques.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:16.780
Medical AI teams should add targeted bias audits to their evaluation pipelines before deployment.

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:29.720
Cisco announced a company-wide rollout of AI agents to its full workforce of 90,000 people. The deployment focuses on internal productivity tools and process automation across departments.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:40.220
Early economics show per employee cost reductions that scale with usage volume. The rollout includes monitoring layers to track agent actions and spending.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:48.620
Organizations evaluating enterprise agent platforms should review Cisco's reported integration patterns and monitoring approach.

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:57.300
Time magazine has begun serving advertisements explicitly designed to be read and acted upon by AI agents rather than humans.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:02.560
The ads target agent decision making loops in browsing and research workflows.

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:08.560
This development shows how content publishers are adapting to agent mediated consumption.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:15.460
The format uses structured prompts that agents are more likely to parse and follow than traditional banner copy.

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:22.320
Teams building web agents should add filters for sponsored content that may attempt to steer agent behavior.

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:29.220
Attackers have created more than 800 counterfeit AI agent skills that deliver malware when installed.

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:35.900
The campaign exploits agent marketplaces and skill registries by mimicking legitimate tool descriptions.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:42.340
Once activated, the fake skills exfiltrate data or execute unauthorized actions.

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:47.800
The operation relies on volume and rapid re-registration after takedowns.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:54.700
Agent developers should implement stricter vetting and sandboxing for third-party skills before allowing execution.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:06:02.020
A new tutorial demonstrates how to wrap a stateful land graph agent in a production-ready streamlit interface.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:08.400
The guide covers session-state management, tool-call visualization, and streaming responses.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:16.860
Developers can follow the provided code to expose land graph agents through a simple web UI without additional frontend frameworks.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:24.480
Submissions are now open for the real-time conversational agent's workshop at NURP's 2026 in Sydney.

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:32.860
The workshop focuses on streaming speech, full duplex dialogue, turn-taking, and evaluation of live interactive systems.

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:39.820
Full papers, short papers, and demo submissions are accepted with a deadline of August 29th.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:46.840
Topics include latency-aware generation, natural interaction metrics, and safety considerations for real-time agents.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:50.700
Okay, let's pop the hood on the auto mode defenses.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:07:02.160
Everyone treats auto mode as a single-safety toggle, but the engineering reality is a layered set of run-time checks and policy models that run alongside the main agent loop.

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:14.340
The core mechanism replaces user-approval prompts with an always-on secondary model that scores proposed actions against a narrow set of disallowed patterns before any tool executes.

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:27.180
This adds measurable latency, roughly one extra forward pass per decision, but removes confirmation fatigue that causes humans to approve 86% of harmful commands in the reported tests.

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:41.080
The defense works best when the policy model has no access to the same untrusted context the primary agent sees, creating an information barrier that stops many injected instructions from reaching the decision stage.

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:56.800
Trade-offs appear at scale. The policy model must stay small enough to keep total cost low, yet large enough to catch novel attack phrases, and the gap between the two sizes is where the remaining 11% of attacks still slip through.

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:02.420
The approach also assumes the set of dangerous actions can be enumerated in advance.

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:19.820
Open-ended coding agents that can install arbitrary packages quickly exceed that assumption. When the policy model is kept deliberately smaller than the main agent, novel phrasing attacks become harder to block without raising false positive rates that frustrate users.

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:30.820
Teams should adopt this pattern only when they can afford the extra inference cost, and when their agent's tool set is narrow enough that disallowed actions can be enumerated in advance.

00:08:30.820 --> 00:08:48.040
For open-ended coding agents, the same approach quickly becomes incomplete without additional sandboxing. A practical next step is to run the policy model on a separate, smaller instance so that even if the primary context is poisoned, the decision layer remains clean.

00:08:49.040 --> 00:09:03.420
This development sits within the ongoing work on agent reliability that we covered yesterday, moving the open question of long horizon safety forward by showing concrete numbers on what a secondary policy model can and cannot catch.

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:12.000
Test-clawed code auto mode on a non-production repository to measure how often it blocks actions you would have approved manually.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:23.780
Review the 16-viable AI-generated bacterial-fage genomes released with the EVO paper if you work on genome scale modeling or synthetic biology validation pipelines.

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:33.380
Add streamlit to an existing LANGGRAPH agent following the new tutorial to get a quick internal demo interface without building a separate frontend.

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:44.180
Submit a short paper or demo to the real-time conversational agent's workshop if your team has latency or turn-taking results ready by August 29th.

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:56.920
Nuriip's 2026 workshop submission deadline for real-time conversational agents is August 29th AOE, andthropic plans to release additional auto mode evaluations in the coming weeks.

